; The project verdict aliases, once, at the top of the test tree.
;
; In your own project these are called `cover`, `mutate` and `admit` —
; the names windtrap's manual, skill, and own root dune use. They are
; renamed `example-*` HERE for one reason: this example lives inside
; windtrap's tree, all three of those aliases are recursive there, and
; same-named aliases in a subdirectory would silently widen the project
; aggregate every time someone ran one at the root.
;
; The two are asymmetric on purpose: coverage accumulates as a side
; effect of any instrumented run, so the cover alias both runs the
; suites and merges; a mutation VERDICT only exists if a run was asked
; to test mutants (WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1), so the mutate alias merges what
; previous runs left. (universe) is load-bearing in both: the .coverage
; and .mutants files are written by test executables at exit and are
; not declarable dependencies, so it makes the milliseconds-cheap merge
; re-run on every build.

(rule
 (alias example-cover)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (run %{bin:windtrap} coverage --min 80)))

(rule
 (alias example-mutate)
 (deps (universe))
 (action
  (run %{bin:windtrap} mutate)))

; The admission alias completes the trio, and is the only one that runs
; rather than merges: WINDTRAP_MUTATE=admit with no filter judges every
; test the run executes, so it asks "can every test in this suite fail?"
; with nothing to keep in sync, and an admission run persists nothing,
; so there is nothing to merge after it.
;
; test_slug only, and the omission is deliberate. test_stats carries the
; deliberately weak law that is the manual's living specimen ("Admitting
; a test"): UNJUSTIFIED by construction, so an alias over it would be
; red forever, and a scaffold whose alias is red teaches that a red
; alias is normal — the opposite of what UNJUSTIFIED means. The specimen
; keeps its own command, in the README, where the red is the lesson. In
; your own project this alias names every unit executable, because
; nothing in yours is a specimen.
;
; Unscoped, unlike that command, because no one prefix is right in both
; places: WINDTRAP_MUTATE_ONLY=examples/x-blueprint/lib is what excludes
; the framework's own sites here, and it matches nothing once this
; directory is copied out, where windtrap arrives as a dependency and
; carries no instrumentation at all. Wide is the safe direction — here
; the suite admits 9 of 9 where the scoped command reports 8 admitted
; and 1 no sites, and every ruling is still true about the test it
; names.

(rule
 (alias example-admit)
 (deps (universe) unit/test_slug.exe)
 (action
  (setenv
   WINDTRAP_MUTATE
   admit
   (run %{exe:unit/test_slug.exe}))))
